{"items":[{"tags":["javascript","jquery","http","fetch-api"],"question_score":574,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":58437909,"is_answered":false,"question_id":35038857,"item_type":"answer","score":911,"last_activity_date":1718805208,"creation_date":1571333430,"body":"A concise, modern approach:\n\nHow it works: URLSearchParams toString() method will convert the URLSearchParams instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string.\nIt is also legal to leave out the  call like so: . JavaScript automatically calls  when an object is concatenated with a string. This does require future code readers to have a deeper understanding of the language to understand your code.\nA complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\n\nIf you are using/supporting...\n\nIE: Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are polyfills available.\n\nNode: As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the  flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like node-fetch. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at .\n\nTypeScript: The values provided to  will be auto-coerced into a string, which is why TypeScript&#39;s definition for  requires you to provide all values as strings. Those who don&#39;t use TypeScript may be comfortable providing other value types to this function if they feel the coercion behavior is intuitive. For examples, numbers will behave exactly as you would expect, while the way arrays get implicitly coerced may cause confusion to future readers.\n\nTypeScript v4 or older with Node: Older versions of TypeScript did not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams in the Node environment. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the  module. This later got resolved at some point during v4 (I&#39;m not sure what the exact version is that fixes this). See this issue for more info.\n\n\n","excerpt":"A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:\n\r\n\r\n// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// <span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose. &hellip; async function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    &#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/comments?&#39; &hellip; ","title":"Setting query string using Fetch GET request"},{"tags":["query-string","htmx","urlsearchparams"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":78022021,"item_type":"question","score":3,"last_activity_date":1718805066,"creation_date":1708358751,"body":"Very simple landing page, thought it would be a good place to start playing with HTMX. The objective is really simple:\nLanding page with a query string in the URL. onLoad it fires a request to server, sending the params from the query string along. Server does its thing and sends back a success or error HTML snippet.\nI do not need a button or form, all the info I need comes from the queryString. Users get redirected to this page from another app.\nQuestion 1: How can I get the searchParms into the ?\nI must be missing something but I couldn&#39;t find any other way than using some JS.\nHTMX:\n\nJS:\n\nSample:\nhttps://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-9z2kgg?file=index.html\nQuestion 2: I would have thought the entire idea behind HTMX was that for the very largest part I don&#39;t need to write the JS.\nOr is this a case where I would look at Hyperscript?\nQuestion 3:  on a div looks odd to me, surely that&#39;s not exactly how it&#39;s thought to be done?\nIn production I would of course do the entire parsing of the queryString and writing the HTML response in the backend - but for that I need to get the params from the queryString back there first. What am I missing?\n","excerpt":"HTMX:\n&lt;div\n  hx-get=&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users&quot;\n  hx-vals=&quot;js:{id: id, comment: comment}&quot;\n  hx-trigger=&quot;load&quot;\n  hx-target=&quot;#result&quot;\n&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div id=&quot;result&quot; class=&quot;m-8 p-8 bg-slate-200 &hellip; &quot;&gt;Loading…&lt;/div&gt;\n\nJS:\nfunction getParams() {\n  // const urlParams = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(window.location.search);\n  const urlParams = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(&#39;id=4&amp;comment=25&#39;);\n  const id = urlParams.get(&#39; &hellip; ","title":"HTMX - Read URL Search Params / Query String and pass into fetch request"},{"tags":["javascript","reactjs","typescript","next.js","server-side-rendering"],"question_score":5,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":77973830,"is_answered":false,"question_id":77971327,"item_type":"answer","score":5,"last_activity_date":1707645922,"creation_date":1707582497,"body":"If you want your page to be a server component, the only way to have a filter is to reload the page with something in the URL, a query string for example.\nStill, to update the URL, you would need a client component while keeping the page itself as a server component.\nFor example, you can do so:\n\n\n","excerpt":"useRouter();\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n  const searchParams = useSearchParams();\n\n  const createQueryString = useCallback(\n    (name: string, value: string) =&gt; {\n      const params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; SetQueryFilters&quot;;\n\nconst RaceResultsPage = async ({\n  params,\n  searchParams,\n}: {\n  params: { raceId: string };\n  searchParams: { filter: string };\n}) =&gt; {\n  const result = await fetch(&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"Server side component list filtering in Next.js"},{"tags":["jquery","loops","filter","comments","url-parameters"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":75651369,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1678465427,"creation_date":1678108849,"body":"I have a blog, and this is the second page, you can get here with a link from the first page. That is why I used the . Now, from the the first JSON, I get the posts. For every post there is a number of comments that you can get by the ID. I have to show for every post the already existing comments. There is 100 posts and 500 comments. Here is my code :\n\nI tried to filter them, but it won&#39;t work because they are not in the same AJAX, I guess.\n","excerpt":"That is why I used the <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>. Now, from the the first JSON, I get the posts. For every post there is a number of comments that you can get by the ID. &hellip; Here is my code :\n    $(function () {\n \n      $.ajax({\n        type: &quot;GET&quot;,\n        url: &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts&quot;,\n        dataType: &#39;json&#39;,\n        success: function (result) { &hellip; ","title":"How do I filter a json file to match with another json&#39;s id?"},{"tags":["svelte","sveltekit"],"question_score":4,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":75220435,"item_type":"question","score":4,"last_activity_date":1674560175,"creation_date":1674557451,"body":"I am trying to use the load() function in the +page.js file to fetch data, which works fine after the initial load of the page. Though if I refresh the page (or change the code and it auto-refreshes) it gives me an error: &quot;500 Internal Error&quot;.\nI can navigate to another route on my page and go back to this page and it works again.\n+page.js:\n\n+page.svelte:\n\nWhat am I doing wrong?\n","excerpt":"+page.js:\nexport async function load() {\n    const res = await fetch(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts&#39;);\n    const books = await res.json();\n\n    return {\n        books\n    }\n}\n\n+page.svelte &hellip; :\n&lt;script&gt;\n    export let data\n    let id = parseInt(new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(window.location.search).get(&#39;id&#39;)) - 1\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;h2&gt;{data.books[id].title}&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;{data.books[id].body}&lt;/p&gt;\n\nWhat am I doing &hellip; ","title":"SvelteKit: +page.js load() function causes &quot;500 Internal Error&quot; after refreshing the page"},{"tags":["javascript","reactjs"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":false,"question_id":74487395,"item_type":"question","score":-1,"last_activity_date":1668790628,"creation_date":1668763282,"body":"I have a json data and I wanted to know how I can access the values using fetch api so that I can print it on the screen.\nthe json data from jsonplaceholder .\nthe page I want it to be displayed\n\n.\nthe code of that page.\n\n","excerpt":"import React, { useEffect, useState } from &#39;react&#39;;\n\nfunction More() {\n    const [item, setItem] = useState();\n    const queryParams = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(window.location.search);\n    const id = queryParams.get &hellip; (&quot;id&quot;);\n    const fetchData = () =&gt; {\n        fetch(`https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos? &hellip; ","title":"I have a json data and I wanted to know how I can access the values using fetch api?"},{"tags":["javascript","reactjs"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":74487535,"is_answered":false,"question_id":74487395,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1668764596,"creation_date":1668763959,"body":"Please use  and  in your code and  remove \n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\n","excerpt":"Please use const queryParams and const id in your code and  remove const id = 1;\n\r\n\r\nconst App = () =&gt; {\n    const [item, setItem] = React.useState({});\n    // const queryParams = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(window.location.search &hellip; );\n    // const id = queryParams.get(&quot;id&quot;);\n    const id = 1;\n    const fetchData = () =&gt; {\n        fetch(`https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos? &hellip; ","title":"I have a json data and I wanted to know how I can access the values using fetch api?"},{"tags":["javascript","reactjs","drop-down-menu","prisma"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":72063750,"is_answered":false,"question_id":72061931,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1651268930,"creation_date":1651266934,"body":"You could use custom hooks to do this.\nThe key is that in your code the second dropdown should watch the changes in the date of the first dropdown &amp; react to these changes. In React you do this by using  (most of the times):\n\nIn the snippet,\n\nThe &quot;states&quot; API is querying a real source of data\nI mocked the counties API (I couldn&#39;t find a free/freesource solution)\nI added a simple cache mechanism for the counties, so the API doesn&#39;t get queried if the data has already been downloaded\n\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\n","excerpt":"setStates] = useState([]);\n\n  const fetchStates = () =&gt; {\n    const myHeaders = new Headers();\n    myHeaders.append(&quot;Content-Type&quot;, &quot;application/x-www-form-urlencoded&quot;);\n\n    const urlencoded = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; setCounties] = useState([]);\n\n  const fetchCounties = (state) =&gt; {\n    if (state in countiesByState) {\n      setCounties(countiesByState[state]);\n    } else if (state) {\n      fetch(&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to make one dropdown menu dependent on another"},{"tags":["php","forms"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":71144902,"is_answered":false,"question_id":71144569,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1645028017,"creation_date":1645026254,"body":"Below you can see how the search string is generated from the form before it is sent to the php script for processing. Admittedly, the search string only makes sense for a GET request. Nonetheless it illustrates, that clearly no &quot;ON&quot; value is sent but instead something like :\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\n","excerpt":"is sent but instead something like usertype=5:\n\r\n\r\n// submit handler de-activated again:\nif (false) document.querySelector(&quot;form&quot;).onsubmit=function(ev){\n ev.preventDefault();\n console.log((new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; (new FormData(this))).toString())\n}\r\n&lt;form  class=&quot;needs-validation&quot; action=&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot; name=&quot;activity&quot; autocomplete=&quot;off&quot;&gt;\n&lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot; id=&quot;radio1 &hellip; ","title":"Radio button with defined value always returns &quot;on&quot;"},{"tags":["angular"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":4,"is_answered":true,"question_id":50448765,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1604288212,"creation_date":1526905117,"body":"I am Using JSONPlace holder Fake API for fetching data. I am getting the api data in service but if I call that service from my app component I am getting  why this is my code \n\nMy Service.ts is\n\n\n\nMy component code is \n\n\n\nwhy I am not able to get service data in component?\n","excerpt":"the api data in service but if I call that service from my app component I am getting undefined why this is my code \n\nMy Service.ts is\n\nimport { Injectable } from &#39;@angular/core&#39;;\nimport { Http, <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> &hellip; , Response, RequestOptions, Headers } from &#39;@angular/http&#39;;\nimport &#39;rxjs/RX&#39;;\nimport &#39;rxjs/add/operator/map&#39;;\n@Injectable()\nexport class Service {\n    apiRoot: string = &#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"Why angular Service data is get undefined if I call from component"},{"tags":["javascript","jquery"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":64128368,"is_answered":false,"question_id":64128203,"item_type":"answer","score":2,"last_activity_date":1601418510,"creation_date":1601417722,"body":"I replaced your code with using fetch instead of . I also updated the append loop to create the TR as an element then append that as a child of the table.\nYou will want to change the URL and  to \n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\n","excerpt":"You will want to change the URL and element.body to element.nombre\n\r\n\r\nthe_url = &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts&quot;    \nparams = { usuario: user};\n\nvar url = new URL(the_url)\n\nurl.search = new &hellip; <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>(params).toString();\n\nfetch(url).then(response =&gt; response.json())\n  .then(data =&gt; {\n\n    data.forEach(element =&gt; {\n      var body = document.querySelector(&#39;table tbody&#39;);\n      tr = document.createElement &hellip; ","title":"JavaScript equivalent to JQuery AJAX petition?"},{"tags":["angular","rest","angular5"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":48598022,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1532502728,"creation_date":1517665450,"body":"I am trying to consume below API (this is opensource ) from angular 5 App: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users\n\n\n\nbelow is my component\n\n\n\nPlease suggest some sample code to call the API in angular5\n","excerpt":"I am trying to consume below API (this is opensource ) from angular 5 App: https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users\n\n\n\nbelow is my component\n\nimport { Component, OnInit,Injectable, NgModule } from &#39;@ &hellip; /login&#39;;\nimport {HttpModule, Http, <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>,,Response, Headers, RequestOptions} from &#39;@angular/http&#39;;\nimport { HttpClient,HttpHeaders, HttpParams, HttpResponse } from &#39;@angular/common/http&#39;;\nimport &hellip; ","title":"unable to call rest api in angular5"},{"tags":["angular","jsonp"],"question_score":5,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":45712555,"is_answered":false,"question_id":44077431,"item_type":"answer","score":3,"last_activity_date":1506107823,"creation_date":1502883447,"body":"Use    instead of  !\n\n\n","excerpt":"import { Injectable } from &#39;@angular/core&#39;;\nimport { Http, Headers, Jsonp, <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> } from &#39;@angular/http&#39;;\nimport &#39;rxjs/add/operator/map&#39;;\n\n@Injectable()\nexport class MyServerService{\n\n    constructor &hellip; (private jsonp: Jsonp){}\n\n    getServers(term: string) {\n                let url = `https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts`;\n\n                let params = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>();\n\n                params.set &hellip; ","title":"Angular 4: JSONP injected script did not invoke callback"},{"tags":["angular","rest","http","url-parameters"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":false,"question_id":45847304,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1503516629,"creation_date":1503514057,"body":"Goal is to produce the url like below:\n\n\n\nI have the following function in my Typescript!\n\n\n\nAny suggestions on how I could produce this dynamic url from the incoming config which is PowerPlantListConfig\n\nThe PowerPlantListConfig is as below:\n\n\n\nEDIT: As a quick and dirty try, this is what I tried (by hard coding values)\n\n\n\nBut that seems not to take effect as I see the URL without the URL parameters getting called!\n","excerpt":"allPowerPlants(config: PowerPlantListConfig): Observable&lt;PowerPlant[]&gt; {\n    // Convert any filters over to Angular&#39;s <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>\n    const params: <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span> = new <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>();\n\n    Object.keys &hellip; // this.http.request(&#39;http://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts/1&#39;)\n    .subscribe((res: Response) =&gt; {\n      console.log(JSON.stringify(res.json()));\n      this.data = JSON.stringify(res.json()); &hellip; ","title":"Angular 4 Populate Query Parameters Dynamically"},{"tags":["javascript","arrays","reactjs","axios"],"question_score":15,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":44402712,"is_answered":false,"question_id":44402079,"item_type":"answer","score":22,"last_activity_date":1496872430,"creation_date":1496803803,"body":"First thing you should do is to check whether links array contains actually contains valid links.\n\nThe following code works for me:\n\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\n\nAlternatively, you can also use fetch and promises\n\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\n","excerpt":"=typeof <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>&amp;&amp;e instanceof <span class=\"highlight\">URLSearchParams</span>}function w(e){return e.replace(/^\\s*/,&quot;&quot;).replace(/\\s*$/,&quot;&quot;)}function v(){return(&quot;undefined&quot;==typeof navigator||&quot;ReactNative&quot;! &hellip; /posts&#39;, &#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/comments&#39;];\r\n\r\naxios.all(linksArr.map(l =&gt; axios.get(l)))\r\n  .then(axios.spread(function (...res) {\r\n    // all requests are now complete\r\n    console.log &hellip; ","title":"How to make multiple Axios requests using a dynamic array of links in Javascript?"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":235}